        "id": "lem-qc-tqc-special-unitary-connected",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Special unitary groups are connected",
        "statement_or_definition": "For every integer $n\\ge1$, the matrix group\n$$SU(n)=\\{A\\in M_n(\\mathbb C):A^*A=I,\\ \\det A=1\\}$$\nis path connected.",
        "deps": [
          "thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms",
          "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
          "thm-kernel-and-fibres-of-complex-exponential",
          "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "proof_obligations": [
          "1.1 The matrix $A$ is unitary and therefore normal. By [F1], choose a unitary\nmatrix $Q$ and real phases $\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_n$ such that\n$A=Q\\operatorname{diag}(e^{i\\alpha_1},\\ldots,e^{i\\alpha_n})Q^*$.\nSince $\\det A=1$, [F2] gives $\\sum_j\\alpha_j=2\\pi k$ for some integer $k$.\nReplace $\\alpha_1$ by $\\alpha_1-2\\pi k$; the eigenvalue is unchanged and the\nsum of phases is now zero. This uses only finitely many eigenvalues.\n[given, F1, F2, choose, algebra]",
          "2.1 Define\n$A(t)=Q\\operatorname{diag}(e^{it\\alpha_1},\\ldots,e^{it\\alpha_n})Q^*$ for\n$0\\le t\\le1$. Each $A(t)$ is unitary by [F2], and\n$\\det A(t)=e^{it\\sum_j\\alpha_j}=1$, so $A(t)\\in SU(n)$. The path is continuous by [F3], $A(0)=I$, and $A(1)=A$.\nThus every element is joined to the identity, proving path connectedness.\n[given, F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra] ∎"
        ],
