        "id": "lem-qc-tqc-fibonacci-coefficient-ring-specialization",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Fibonacci coefficient ring and its exact mod-31 specialization",
        "statement_or_definition": "Let $r=e^{-3\\pi i/5}$, $\\phi=(1+\\sqrt5)/2=1-r-r^{-1}$, and\n$s=\\sqrt\\phi>0$. Then\n$$\\mathbb Z[r,s]\\cong\n\\mathbb Z[x,y]\\big/\\bigl(x^4-x^3+x^2-x+1,\\ y^2-(1-x-x^{-1})\\bigr),$$\nwhere $x^{-1}=x^9$. The assignment\n$$r\\mapsto15,\\qquad r^{-1}\\mapsto29,\\qquad\n\\phi\\mapsto19,\\qquad s\\mapsto9$$\ndefines a ring homomorphism to $\\mathbb F_{31}$. The elements $r$ and\n$\\phi$ map to nonzero units, and conjugation of coefficients is handled by\n$r\\mapsto r^{-1}$ before reduction.",
        "deps": [
          "ex-qc-tqc-fibonacci-data"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "proof_obligations": [
          "1.1 The number $r$ is a primitive tenth root of unity, so its minimal\npolynomial is $\\Phi_{10}(x)=x^4-x^3+x^2-x+1$ and $[\\mathbb Q(r):\\mathbb Q]=4$.\nIts maximal real subfield is $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt5)$, and the nontrivial\nconjugate of $\\phi$ is $(1-\\sqrt5)/2=-\\phi^{-1}$; hence the field norm of\n$\\phi$ from $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt5)$ to $\\mathbb Q$ is $-1$. Therefore $\\phi$ is\nnot a square in that real subfield, since the norm of a square would be a\nnonnegative rational square. [given, A1, algebra]",
          "1.2 If $u\\in\\mathbb Q(r)$ satisfied $u^2=\\phi$, complex conjugation would\ngive $\\bar u^2=\\phi$, hence $\\bar u=u$ or $\\bar u=-u$. In the first case\n$u\\in\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt5)$, contradicting step 1.1. In the second case $u$ is\npurely imaginary in the chosen embedding, so $u^2$ is nonpositive real,\ncontrary to $\\phi>0$. Thus $Y^2-\\phi$ is irreducible over $\\mathbb Q(r)$\nand $[\\mathbb Q(r,s):\\mathbb Q]=8$. The displayed monic relations therefore\ngive the full degree-eight presentation; no additional relation is being\nassumed. [given, step 1.1, algebra]",
          "2.1 In $\\mathbb F_{31}$, $15^{-1}=29$,\n$\\Phi_{10}(15)=0$, $1-15-29=19$, and $9^2=19$. Thus the assignments satisfy\nboth defining relations and extend to the stated ring homomorphism. The\nimages $15$ and $19$ are nonzero, so $r,\\phi$ and their powers are units;\nall denominators in the path projectors and normalized braid matrices are\ntherefore invertible after specialization. [given, A1, step 1.2, algebra]",
          "2.2 In the number field, complex conjugation fixes $\\phi,s$ and sends\n$r$ to $r^{-1}$. Therefore a coefficient involving conjugation is first\nrewritten with $r^{-1}$ and only then reduced by $r^{-1}\\mapsto29$.\nNo conjugation operation on $\\mathbb F_{31}$ is required. [given, step 2.1, algebra] ∎"
        ],
